16. Cascading Fallback
The second major responsibility of this module is dispatch resilience.
Suppose the Intelligent Dispatch Engine selects:
AMB-03
The backend sends the assignment.
Now there are three possible outcomes:
                Assignment
                    │
          ┌─────────┼─────────┐
          ▼         ▼         ▼
       ACCEPT     REJECT    TIMEOUT
          │         │         │
          ▼         └────┬────┘
       Continue           │
                          ▼
                     FALLBACK
________________________________________
17. Why Fallback Is Necessary
Consider:
Emergency
   ↓
AMB-03 selected
   ↓
Driver doesn't respond
If the system simply waits:
Emergency request remains pending
This defeats the purpose of an emergency-response platform.
Instead:
AMB-03
  ↓
Timeout
  ↓
Immediately trigger fallback
The request remains active.
The bystander does not have to submit another request.
________________________________________
18. Fallback Trigger Conditions
The fallback mechanism can be triggered by:
Driver rejection
Driver → REJECT
Driver timeout
Assignment sent
       ↓
No response
       ↓
Timeout
Ambulance becoming unavailable
Assigned ambulance
       ↓
Offline / unavailable
The exact timeout duration should be configurable in the simulation.
________________________________________
19. Fallback Process
The complete fallback flow:
Assignment Created
       ↓
Send to Driver
       ↓
Start Response Timer
       ↓
Wait
       ↓
Driver Response?
     /       \
   YES        NO
    │          │
 ACCEPT     TIMEOUT
    │          │
    │          ▼
    │       Mark Failed
    │          │
    │          ▼
    │      Exclude Ambulance
    │          │
    │          ▼
    │    Re-run Dispatch
    │          │
    │          ▼
    │    Select Next Best
    │          │
    │          ▼
    │    Send Assignment
    │
    ▼
Continue
________________________________________
20. Re-running the Intelligent Dispatch Engine
The fallback mechanism should not implement a completely separate ambulance-selection algorithm.
Instead:
Fallback
   ↓
Update request state
   ↓
Exclude failed ambulance
   ↓
Call Intelligent Dispatch Engine
Example:
Initial candidates:

A1
A2
A3
A4

Selected:
A2
A2 rejects.
Excluded:
A2
Engine evaluates:
A1
A3
A4
Suppose A3 is now best.
A3 → assigned
________________________________________
21. Cascading Multiple Failures
The system can continue the process.
Example:
Emergency
   ↓
A2 selected
   ↓
A2 timeout
   ↓
A3 selected
   ↓
A3 rejects
   ↓
A1 selected
   ↓
A1 accepts
This is why it is called:
Cascading Fallback Dispatch
because the assignment cascades from one candidate to another until an ambulance accepts or the candidate pool is exhausted.
________________________________________
22. Attempt History
Every attempt should be recorded.
Example:
Request: UK-0001

Attempt 1
────────────
Ambulance: A2
Time: 10:01:02
Result: TIMEOUT

Attempt 2
────────────
Ambulance: A3
Time: 10:01:14
Result: REJECTED

Attempt 3
────────────
Ambulance: A1
Time: 10:01:27
Result: ACCEPTED
This creates a complete audit trail.
________________________________________
23. request_attempts Concept
A dedicated attempt record can contain:
requestId
attemptNumber
ambulanceId
assignedAt
responseAt
response
failureReason
Example:
{
    requestId: "UK-0001",
    attemptNumber: 2,
    ambulanceId: "AMB-03",
    response: "REJECTED"
}
This also allows the research team to calculate:
Average attempts per request
Fallback frequency
Average fallback delay
Assignment success rate
________________________________________
24. Preventing Duplicate Assignment
A critical backend rule is:
Only one ambulance should be the active responder for a request at a time.
Suppose:
A1 assigned
before A1 responds, the system must not simultaneously assign A2 unless the configured timeout/failure condition has occurred.
After A1 times out:
A1 → failed
A2 → active assignment
The request state should be updated atomically to prevent conflicting assignments.
________________________________________
25. Fallback Candidate Exclusion
Failed candidates must be excluded from the current request.
Example:
Request UK-001

Failed:
A1
A3

Available candidates:
A2
A4
A5
The dispatch engine must not immediately select A1/A3 again.
________________________________________
26. No Ambulance Available
Eventually, the candidate pool may become empty.
A1 → Failed
A2 → Failed
A3 → Failed
A4 → Out of range
Then:
Candidates remaining = 0
The system changes the request to:
NO_AMBULANCE_AVAILABLE
The bystander should receive an appropriate notification.
The simulation should record this as a failed dispatch scenario rather than hiding it.
________________________________________
