30. Response-Time Measurement
The complete response timeline can be captured.
T0 = Emergency request created
T1 = First ambulance assigned
T2 = First driver response / timeout
T3 = Fallback triggered
T4 = Second ambulance assigned
T5 = Driver accepts
T6 = Ambulance starts movement
T7 = Patient reached
Important metrics:
Initial dispatch latency
= T1 - T0

Fallback delay
= T4 - T3

Driver response time
= T5 - T4

Travel time
= T7 - T6

Total response time
= T7 - T0
This is directly useful when evaluating the project's 12-minute target.
________________________________________
31. 12-Minute Target Evaluation
The system should not claim that it automatically achieves 12 minutes.
Instead:
Target
  ↓
≈ 12 minutes
  ↓
Simulation
  ↓
Run multiple scenarios
  ↓
Measure actual response time
  ↓
Optimize
  ↓
Run again
For example, the simulation might produce:
Baseline:
15.2 minutes

UyirKappan:
12.8 minutes
After optimization:
UyirKappan:
11.9 minutes
Only the measured values should be reported as experimental results.
________________________________________
32. Simulation Scenarios
The team should test multiple scenarios.
Scenario A — Normal
Assignment → Accept → Track → Arrival
Scenario B — First Driver Rejects
A1 → Reject
A2 → Accept
Scenario C — First Driver Times Out
A1 → Timeout
A2 → Accept
Scenario D — Multiple Failures
A1 → Timeout
A2 → Reject
A3 → Accept
Scenario E — Traffic Increase
Route selected
       ↓
Traffic increases
       ↓
ETA increases
Scenario F — Road Closure
Current route
       ↓
Road closed
       ↓
Alternative route
       ↓
ETA recalculated
Scenario G — No Available Ambulance
All candidates unavailable
       ↓
No assignment
